The '''bir''' ([[Burawi language|Burawi]]: ''bir'', "iron") was a standardised iron currency used in the southern regions of [[Southwestern Kaftia]] from the early [[Kingdom of Ufyen|Iron Age]] (~1,000 BCE) until its gradual displacement by Mesogean metal coinage following [[Aetolians|Aetolian]] contact (~600 BCE onward). It remained in circulation in the [[Kushish]] and [[Orma]] highlands until approximately the 3rd century BCE.

== Description ==

The ''bir'' took the form of a crescent or half-moon shaped iron blade, approximately 10–15 cm across the outer curve. The two pointed tips of the crescent were slightly flattened, and the inner curve was sharpened to a blunt edge. Each piece was forged from a single billet of {{W|wrought iron}} and weighed between 80 and 120 grams in its standardised form, though earlier examples vary considerably in size.

The crescent shape distinguished the ''bir'' from iron tools such as axeheads or knife blades, marking it as an object produced specifically for exchange rather than practical use. The form also had practical advantages: ''bir'' could be strung on a cord through the open centre of the crescent for transport, and their curved shape allowed them to be stacked and counted efficiently. Hoards of several hundred ''bir'' strung on leather cords have been recovered from ''[[Kaas|kaas]]'' hillforts across the [[Kushish]] highlands.

== History ==

=== Origins ===

The ''bir'' emerged in the [[Lake Haro]] basin and [[Kushish]] highlands during the early [[Kingdom of Ufyen|Ufyen]] period (~1,000–800 BCE), as the region's iron-smelting capacity exceeded the immediate demand for tools and weapons. The earliest examples, recovered from burial sites associated with the [[Kingdom of Ufyen#The Geedi Dynasty|Geedi Dynasty]], are irregular in shape and weight, suggesting that iron was initially exchanged by mass rather than by standardised units.

By approximately 800 BCE, the Geedi administration had imposed a degree of standardisation. ''Bir'' from this period are more uniform in size and bear simple incised marks — typically a single line or cross — that may indicate the smithy of origin or a quality assurance stamp. The standardisation of the ''bir'' coincided with Ufyen's territorial expansion, as the currency facilitated the collection of tribute and the payment of garrisons across the growing empire.

=== Peak circulation ===

During the late Geedi period (~800–600 BCE), the ''bir'' was the dominant medium of exchange across the entire territory controlled by Ufyen, from the [[Kushish]] highlands to the [[Mesogean Sea]] coast. Archaeological evidence indicates that ''bir'' circulated alongside barter exchange rather than replacing it: grain, cattle, and salt remained important units of value for everyday transactions, while ''bir'' were used for larger payments such as tribute, bride-price, and the purchase of prestige goods.

The ''bir'' also entered the [[Northern Basin]] in limited quantities through trade across the [[Great Kaftian Mountain Range]] passes, though it never displaced the [[Cowrie|cowrie shell]] as the primary northern currency. Northern tumulus burials occasionally contain small numbers of ''bir'', interpreted as prestige items rather than evidence of widespread monetary use.

=== Decline ===

The arrival of [[Aetolians|Aetolian]] traders on the southern coast around 600 BCE introduced {{W|coinage}} — stamped metal discs of gold, silver, and bronze whose value was guaranteed by an issuing authority. Mesogean coins offered several advantages over the ''bir'': they were made of rarer metals (giving higher value per unit of weight), their stamped designs made them difficult to counterfeit, and they were readily accepted across the Mesogean trade network.

The ''bir'' was displaced first on the coast, where Mesogean coins became the standard of trade by approximately 400 BCE. In the highlands, the ''bir'' persisted longer: the [[Kingdom of Ufyen#The Dalmar restoration|Dalmar Dynasty]] continued to collect tariffs in ''bir'' and to pay its garrisons with iron currency until approximately the 3rd century BCE, when even the highland economy had become sufficiently integrated with Mesogean commerce to make coin the preferred medium.

== Legacy ==

The word ''bir'' survives in modern [[Burawi language|Burawi]] as the general term for "iron" and in several idiomatic expressions relating to wealth and value. The phrase ''bir la'aan'' ("without iron") is a colloquial term for poverty, and ''bir badan'' ("much iron") denotes affluence — both reflecting the currency's long association with economic status in the southern highlands.

Archaeological collections of ''bir'' are held in the national museums of [[Burawa]], [[Kushish]], and [[Orma]]. The largest known hoard, containing over 1,200 ''bir'' strung on original leather cords, was excavated from a Geedi-era ''kaas'' near [[Jowhar]] in 1987.

